      "id": "graph-convolution-operator-and-physical-claim",
      "skill": "engineering-application-journal-writing-skill",
      "prompt": "Please review a fictional engineering method description. Two independently published sources explicitly update node features using a normalized adjacency matrix, learned feature projections and a nonlinearity. One uses variable-time nodes, the other process-variable nodes. The current model implements the same general operation on process-variable features with learned similarity edges. It calls this graph convolution, then claims its edge weights prove physical fault propagation and a causal process topology. Decide which base terminology is supported and give a concise method sentence preserving the actual operation. No intervention or physical direction evidence has been supplied. Do not use the two-source rule to declare identical graph meanings or established field-wide prevalence.",
      "required_behaviors": [
        "Retain graph convolution as the shared base operation.",
        "Separate actual node/edge definitions and statistical graph computation from physical causal claims."
      ],
      "forbidden_behaviors": [
        "Infer fault propagation or causal identification from learned similarity or two papers."
      ],
      "outcome": "pass",
      "language": "en",
